[Core Chinese Characters] {Two large Chinese characters}
[Calligraphy Title] {Four-character cultural theme}
[English Subtitle] {English theme name}
[Cultural Line Drawing Elements] {3–5 related figures, buildings, artifacts, or natural landscapes}
[Auxiliary Colophon] {3–5 groups of short Chinese phrases, can be automatically created}
[Color Scheme] {Default Vermillion Red, Cyan Blue, Ink Black, Warm Cream White, a small amount of Ancient Gold}
[Ratio] 9:16 Vertical

Create a high-completion Oriental experimental typography art poster, blending giant calligraphy character deconstruction, retro multi-color screen printing, traditional cultural line drawing, and modern editorial layout, presenting a bold, intense, and culturally rich visual style.

I. Giant Character Deconstruction

Enlarge the two core Chinese characters to occupy about 80% of the screen height, using thick, unrestrained, and powerful calligraphy strokes. The character shapes are exaggerated, stretched, interlaced, overlapped, and partially cropped, forming a strong abstract geometric composition while maintaining basic legibility. The stroke movement changes according to the theme, possessing a natural calligraphic rhythm and visual momentum.

II. Three-Color Overlay Interspersing

Form huge organic color blocks with Vermillion Red, Cyan Blue, and Ink Black. The three colors intersperse, cut, block, and nest within the calligraphy strokes, utilizing the cream-white negative space to form a rich figure-ground relationship. The colors are vivid and saturated with sharp edges, possessing the texture of a combination of flat screen printing and traditional calligraphy. Avoid simple colored text layering and 3D shadows.

III. Oriental Typography Layout

Arrange an ancient gold vertical calligraphy title in the upper left of the screen, accompanied by a fine English subtitle. Distributed at the top, sides, and partial white space areas are multiple groups of miniature vertical Chinese colophons, using Songti, Mingti, and a small amount of calligraphy fonts, paired with 2–4 vermillion seals, fine line separators, and partial English vertical arrangement. Emphasize the scale contrast between giant calligraphy and miniature text; the text has real cultural meaning, and the layout is rich and well-organized, presenting a mature Oriental editorial design aesthetic.

IV. Precise Cultural Line Drawing

In the lower approximately one-third area of the screen, integrate traditional figures, buildings, animals, plants, and artifacts related to the theme into the giant characters. Use exquisite white traditional line drawings to depict rich clothing patterns, architectural structures, and natural details, forming a narrative classical woodcut effect. Add a small amount of white geometric fine-line frames to naturally connect different scenes. Illustrations and red-blue-black color blocks intersperse with each other rather than being arranged independently, presenting a complex and unified visual structure.

V. Overall Visual Requirements

Adopt an asymmetrical, high-tension free composition. Giant characters dominate the vision, miniature colophons are scattered around the edges, and precise line drawings are embedded below, forming a clear information hierarchy. The background is a clean warm cream white, and the overall style maintains a flat, matte, high-contrast screen printing aesthetic. Seen from afar, it has strong graphic impact; seen up close, it has rich cultural details. Each poster should have a complete theme, real copy, and independent artistic expression while maintaining a unified series style.

