Please design a set of 4 complete Eastern contemporary culture posters around the same [Theme]. The theme, core text, and basic exhibition information of the four posters must remain consistent, but they must respectively adopt 4 sets of clearly different graphic design languages. The focus is not simply on changing the color scheme, but on changing the composition, font scale, image organization, information structure, material, and visual rhythm, allowing people to see at a glance that these are "four design solutions for the same theme."

The overall reference should be mature Eastern culture exhibitions, art museum visuals, editorial design, and contemporary graphic poster aesthetics, combining traditional Eastern elements with modern design language. Each poster must be a complete work that can stand alone, with clear thematic expression, main visual, auxiliary images, text hierarchy, and information system; do not make them template diagrams with only titles and backgrounds.

The first type is [Ink Deconstruction Style]: Use warm white Xuan paper, large areas of negative space, giant free calligraphy, ancient painting fragments, water ripples, mountain shapes, clouds, and ink marks for asymmetrical collage. Giant Chinese calligraphy directly serves as the compositional skeleton, forming occlusion, cropping, and interleaving with ancient paintings, water ripples, and ink marks, while adding a small amount of modern Chinese and English editorial information and cinnabar seals. The overall color palette is mainly black, white, and gray, with visual freedom and tension, possessing a sense of Eastern experimental art and modern editorial design.

The second type is [Modern Exhibition Style]: Adopt strict modern editorial grids, over-scaled Chinese characters, horizontal image sample strips, and a sophisticated small information system. Reorganize Eastern natural materials, ancient painting details, patterns, artifacts, or photographic images like museum research archives. Colors can use restrained combinations such as off-white, dark green, celadon gray-green, and cream yellow, letting the giant text first become a geometric composition and then be read as the theme. The overall style should have the professional visual sense of cultural institutions, art museums, and design exhibitions.

The third type is [Mineral Color Giant Word Style]: Use Eastern mineral colors such as stone blue, malachite green, ochre gold, gray blue, and beige to form a nearly full-bleed landscape color field. Form continuous visual momentum through elements like rivers, clouds, and mountains, then overlay over-scaled ivory white calligraphy. The text must truly engage in occlusion, interleaving, and front-to-back spatial relationships with the landscape, rather than simply being pasted on the background. Seen from afar, one first sees a strong color field and giant text; seen up close, one then discovers natural landscapes and details.

The fourth type is [Black Background Deconstruction Style]: Use a deep charcoal black background, giant ivory white modern Chinese fonts, Eastern artifacts, rocks, flowing cloud patterns, ancient painting silhouettes, and continuous curves for collage, then add a restrained geometric frame, thin lines, exhibit annotations, and a museum-archive-style information system. The overall palette is mainly black, ivory white, celadon gray-green, old gold, and very little cinnabar, making traditional cultural materials present the visual temperament of contemporary art exhibitions, design biennials, and high-end cultural institutions.

The four posters must maintain a clear series sense: the theme is completely consistent, the core Chinese and English titles are consistent, the basic exhibition information is consistent, and a small number of Eastern cultural identification elements are uniformly retained. However, the four must form significant differences in composition, visual focus, font expression, color system, image materials, and information organization, and cannot just be the same template with changed colors or backgrounds.

Each poster establishes a clear three-level visual hierarchy: the first level is a sufficiently strong large main visual or giant theme text; the second level is Eastern cultural images, landscapes, artifacts, ancient paintings, or other thematic visual materials; the third level is Chinese and English exhibition information, date, venue, explanatory text, and a small number of seals. Seen from afar, there must be a clear large shape and visual focus; seen up close, one then discovers rich text-image relationships and design details.

Please ensure the works have sufficient information density, layout design sense, and completeness. Do not cut corners, do not only generate "background image + centered title"; real processing of the interleaving, occlusion, cropping, proportion, negative space, hierarchy, and reading path between text and image is required, making each one look like a cultural poster fully designed by a professional graphic designer.

Avoid cheap Guochao, ancient style game UIs, travel promotion posters, ordinary landscape paintings with direct text addition, four images only with color changes, excessive symmetry, full-screen small elements, random decorations, meaningless English, gibberish text, numbering, and four-pointed stars.

