Create a 9:16 high-end youth fashion editorial poster with an asymmetric split layout. Keep a clean white editorial column on the left, around 25% of the canvas, and a large color portrait field on the right. Use a close-up fashion portrait with distinctive styling, realistic skin texture, natural hair detail, statement eyewear and metallic accessories.

Keep the left editorial column moderately dense with short headlines, keywords and compact editorial copy. Build hierarchy through spacing, scale and font weight rather than black divider lines or heavy borders.

Place [MAIN WORD] vertically across the boundary between the white column and the portrait field, using oversized stacked letters as the main visual spine.

The typography must use an image-filled glitch-edge effect. Fill each giant letter with the exact same continuous portrait photograph and background instead of a flat color, white fill or transparent hollow interior. Where the letters extend into the white column, the photographic fill becomes clearly visible. Where they overlap the portrait field, the internal image naturally merges back into the original photograph.

Define the letters with very thin, clean white outlines. Keep around 80–90% of each letter edge crisp and intact, while applying controlled glitch distortion to only 10–20% of selected edges.

Use subtle horizontal scan slices, tiny lateral displacement, stepped pixel breaks, repeated outline fragments and small strips of the photographic fill shifted slightly sideways. Keep the glitch restrained, graphic and mostly monochrome. Do not use RGB color separation, full-frame distortion, cyberpunk noise or excessive digital artifacts.

The portrait should remain clearly readable and visually dominant. Let the oversized letters overlap the portrait without destroying the face. Use distinctive youth-fashion styling such as expressive hair, statement eyewear, layered metallic jewelry and a strong but believable outfit.

Add a large ultra-thin [SECONDARY HEADLINE] in the upper-right and a bold plus thin secondary title combination near the lower-right. Maintain strong macro and micro typography contrast, clear editorial grid structure and enough breathing room.

Use a cohesive limited palette based on [BACKGROUND COLOR], white, black, natural skin tones and metallic accents.

The final image should feel like a contemporary independent fashion magazine cover: bold, youthful, highly graphic and instantly readable, with the oversized image-filled letters acting as the defining visual effect.

Avoid ordinary outline text, transparent hollow letters, flat-color giant type, excessive copy, black horizontal divider lines, long black vertical rules, heavy borders, full-screen glitch, random digital noise, plastic skin, CGI texture, numbered issue labels, starbursts and sparkle symbols.

